The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (change_percpu)
    mvpp2_bm_pool_update_priv_fc(priv, false);
```
```
if (change_percpu)
    mvpp2_bm_pool_update_priv_fc(priv, true);
```
Reason: These calls are executed unconditionally when `change_percpu` is true, but `mvpp2_bm_pool_update_priv_fc` accesses `priv->cm3_base` via `mvpp2_cm3_read()`/`mvpp2_cm3_write()`, which is NULL if the CM3 SRAM is missing. Other call sites guard with `priv->global_tx_fc`, but this one does not.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (change_percpu && priv->global_tx_fc)
    mvpp2_bm_pool_update_priv_fc(priv, false);
```
```
if (change_percpu && priv->global_tx_fc)
    mvpp2_bm_pool_update_priv_fc(priv, true);
```
Reason: Adding the check `priv->global_tx_fc` prevents the hardware access when global TX flow control is disabled (which also means `priv->cm3_base` is NULL or irrelevant), avoiding the NULL pointer dereference. This is consistent with the guard used elsewhere in the driver.

